## THE CLAIMS (to attack, never proof). Line numbers are at the head, MEASURED 16:21; re-measure
- **API locks (`UatOpenAuthentication.cs:90-123`).** Off when `Auth:UatOpen:Enabled` is false (`:94`: nothing registered). On: label must be `uat` ordinal (`:95-97`); if ANY of `HostingPlatformMarkers` (`DevelopmentAuthentication.cs:31`: `WEBSITE_INSTANCE_ID`, `WEBSITE_SITE_NAME`, `CONTAINER_APP_NAME`, `KUBERNETES_SERVICE_HOST`) is set, `WEBSITE_SITE_NAME` must contain `-uat-` (`:98-100`); not Development (`:101`); no Development sign-in (`:103`); key not an unresolved `@Microsoft.KeyVault(` and at least 32 UTF-8 bytes (`:105-110`); seed owner oid and tid non-empty, tid not the dev tenant, tid equal the tenant of `Entra:Authority` (`:112-117`). Refusals throw at start-up with messages that name settings, not values. **Judge: the two locks are two settings the same operator writes. Lock 2's platform tie only bites on a hosting platform; with no platform marker the label alone satisfies it. Is that acceptable, and is `WEBSITE_SITE_NAME` really platform-supplied and un-overridable by an app setting (UNMEASURED; B409 brief:108 says B410 measures it)?**
- **Assertion (`:127-143`, `:80`, `:177-198`; web `uatOpen.ts:58-62`).** Shape `v1.<level>.<iat10>.<exp10>.<nonce22>.<sig43>`, anchored with `\z`, at most 256 chars, level in the four, `FixedTimeEquals` on 32 bytes, life at most 120 s, skew 30 s; one header only (`:185`); a present-but-bad header is 401, never a fall-through to Entra (`:151-152` selector sends any request WITH the header to the UatOpen scheme). **The web mints a 60-second life; the API accepts up to 120 s plus 30 s of skew. The nonce is never stored or checked, so a replay INSIDE the window is accepted: the brief's D4 asked for "a random id", not a replay cache. Measure it; report as a finding with the severity the floor gives; do not call it a bug in the builder's terms.**
- **Refusals (`:158-168`; `DestructiveAction.cs`; `AssessmentModule.cs:206,227,229,231,232`).** ONE middleware registered only in open mode (`Program.cs:141`), AFTER follow-up isolation and BEFORE authentication, answering 403 problem+json code `uat.action-off` for any endpoint carrying the `DestructiveAction` metadata: deleteCustomer, resetShowcase, resetDemoData, exportUserPersonalData, eraseUserPersonalData. It reads `GetEndpoint()`, so it needs routing to have run first: prove it by program, not by reading. An unauthenticated caller also gets 403, not 401.
- **Web (`config.ts:72-84`; `uatOpen.ts`; `routes.ts:22`; `proxy.ts:71`; `bff.ts:426-436,458`).** `resolveAuth` returns mode `uat` BEFORE Entra only for switch exactly `on`, `HPSM_ENVIRONMENT` exactly `uat` (read trimmed: `"uat "` passes on the web and fails on the API, RELAYED STATUS:29), hosted ⇒ `WEBSITE_SITE_NAME` contains `-uat-`, no Development header, key and `AUTH_SECRET` usable; a switch that is neither `on` nor `off`/empty is mode `off`. Level cookie `hpsm_uat_level`, sealed (`seal.ts`, purpose `uat-level`), HttpOnly, SameSite=Lax, Secure when hosted or https, Max-Age 28800, server-side age cap 8 h; `/api/auth/uat` is 404 in mock and Entra mode, 503 in `off`. Choose route: Fetch-Metadata then Origin same-origin check (a POST with neither header is allowed), form-urlencoded only, 1 KiB cap while streaming, exactly one `level` from the allow-list, 303 to `/?next=<safeReturnPath(next) ?? landing>` (a deviation from D5's "303 to next", the B386 pattern). **There is NO server-side revocation: sign-out clears the cookie; a copied cookie still works for 8 h** (compare B59 F-1, which made the Entra sign-out revoke).
- **Start page (`UatStartScreen.tsx:20-23,28,38,46`).** The four sentences, heading `HP Security Services Playbook` and lead sentence are literals; `next` re-validated at `:28`, used at `:38` for `router.replace`; hidden `next` field only when valid. `AppShell.tsx` hides "Reset demo" when `auth === "uat"`.
